        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "I have all the information needed. Let me solve the problem.\n\n**Setup:** Parabola $y=x^2+bx+c$ passes through $A(1,1)$ and $B(3,1)$.\n\n**(1)** Since $A$ and $B$ have the same $y$-value, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1+3}{2}=2$, so $-\\frac{b}{2}=2 \\Rightarrow b=-4$. Substituting $A(1,1)$: $1=1-4+c \\Rightarrow c=4$.\n\nSo $y=x^2-4x+4=(x-2)^2$, vertex $P(2,0)$, and $C=(0,4)$.\n\n**(2)** The second parabola $y=ax^2+mx+n$ also passes through $A(1,1)$, $B(3,1)$, so its axis of symmetry is also $x=2$: $-\\frac{m}{2a}=2 \\Rightarrow m=-4a$. Then $y=ax^2-4ax+n$. Substituting $A(1,1)$: $1=a-4a+n \\Rightarrow n=3a+1$.\n\nSo $y=a(x-2)^2+(1-3a)$, vertex $Q(2,\\,1-3a)$, and $D=(0,\\,3a+1)$.\n\n**①** $CD=|(3a+1)-4|=|3a-3|=3|a-1|$. $PQ=|(1-3a)-0|=|1-3a|=|3a-1|$.\n\nSo $\\dfrac{CD}{PQ}=\\dfrac{3|a-1|}{|3a-1|}$.\n\n**②** $C(0,4)$, $D(0,3a+1)$ lie on the $y$-axis, so $CD$ is vertical. $P(2,0)$, $Q(2,1-3a)$ lie on $x=2$, so $PQ$ is vertical. Thus $CD \\parallel PQ$ — they are the two parallel sides (bases) of the trapezoid $CDPQ$.\n\nFor it to be a right trapezoid, one leg must be perpendicular to the bases, i.e., horizontal. The legs are $CP$ and $DQ$.\n\n- $CP$ horizontal $\\Rightarrow y_C=y_P \\Rightarrow 4=0$, impossible.\n- $DQ$ horizontal $\\Rightarrow y_D=y_Q \\Rightarrow 3a+1=1-3a \\Rightarrow 6a=0 \\Rightarrow a=0$.\n\nWith $a=0$: $D(0,1)$, $Q(2,1)$, so $DQ$ is horizontal (length 2), $CD=3$, $PQ=1$. Quadrilateral $CDPQ$ has vertices $C(0,4),D(0,1),Q(2,1),P(2,0)$ — a right trapezoid with right angles at $D$ and $Q$.\n\nInterior angles: at $C$: between $CD$ (downward) and $CP$ (direction $(2,-4)$) — angle $\\angle DCP$. At $P$: $\\angle DPQ$... Let me compute the angles.\n\nAt $C$: vectors $C\\to D=(0,-3)$, $C\\to P=(2,-4)$. $\\cos\\angle C=\\frac{0\\cdot2+(-3)(-4)}{3\\cdot\\sqrt{20}}=\\frac{12}{3\\cdot2\\sqrt5}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$, so $\\angle C\\approx26.57°$.\nAt $P$: vectors $P\\to C=(-2,4)$, $P\\to Q=(0,1)$. $\\cos\\angle P=\\frac{4}{1\\cdot\\sqrt{20}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$, so $\\angle P\\approx26.57°$.\nAngles at $D$ and $Q$ are $90°$.\n\nSmallest interior angle is $\\approx26.57°$, with $\\sin = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{5}$.\n\nLet me verify: $\\sin\\angle C = \\frac{|0\\cdot(-4)-(-3)\\cdot2|}{3\\cdot\\sqrt{20}}=\\frac{6}{3\\cdot2\\sqrt5}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{5}$. ✓",
        "tool_calls": [
